# Working dashboard report controls
Workstream-ID: lea-23
GitHub-Issue: https://github.com/Zanark/LinkedIn-EasyApply-Automaton/issues/23
Publication: PUBLIC_SAFE_PROJECTION
Source-record: Reviewed bounded repair of the dashboard Reports controls
Status: DONE
Milestone: Make exported reports accessible from the dashboard
Dependencies: lea-09

## Purpose
Replace browser-blocked local-file links with usable report browsing inside the
dashboard. Keep native folder opening as a host-side convenience without claiming
that a window was observed. Preserve private inputs and independently running
automation; source-dashboard reload is explicitly permitted for this repair.

## Tasks
- [x] Trace both report controls and distinguish browser access from host-side opening.
- [x] Add an in-dashboard report picker, previews and original-file downloads.
- [x] Check native opener failures and make dispatch-only feedback accurate.
- [x] Exercise both report categories and failure paths with fictional files.
- [x] Update affected usage, architecture and troubleshooting guidance.
- [x] Maintain one issue, Project item and persistent backlink through scoped synchronization.

## Acceptance criteria
- [x] Analysis and Outreach In browser buttons open usable report selection without file:// links.
- [x] Supported text and HTML reports can be previewed; selected original files can be downloaded.
- [x] Missing folders, empty folders and list/read failures have explicit useful feedback.
- [x] Native launch failures are reported; launch dispatch is not described as an observed open window.
- [x] Checks do not open user folders, inspect private reports, restart automation or alter saved setup.
- [x] Existing release binaries, unrelated changes and older tracking mappings remain intact.
- [x] One repository issue, one correctly staged Project item and a durable backlink represent the workstream.

## Completion evidence
Both categories now open a Streamlit dialog with explicit file selection,
HTML/Markdown/UTF-8 previews and original-byte downloads. PDF/XLSX and
non-UTF-8 files remain downloadable. Listing is flat, excludes symbolic links
and rechecks containment before reading only the selected file. Errors are
shown inline. Native feedback confirms a host-side request, not an observed
window; non-Windows commands now check their exit status.

The first real-browser check found a popover overlay blocking dialog clicks
despite passing AppTest cases. The browser-button callback now closes the keyed,
stateful popover before the dialog renders outside it. A fresh headless Edge
context subsequently passes ordinary selection clicks, actual HTML rendering
and opaque-origin separation, Markdown fragment reruns, Outreach text and three
byte-identical downloads. Both fictional dialog screenshots were reviewed.

Final Black, Ruff and mypy gates pass; all 22 focused tests pass, including
opening each category from an initially open popover. Tests use fictional files
and stub native openers. Owned fixture processes and disposable files are
cleaned up. All twenty-two older workstream files/mappings, selected private
input/launcher metadata, HEAD and empty staging are preserved.

The issue/item/backlink identity is independently verified. Declare this
bounded technical repair complete for scoped status reconciliation; final
settled read-back is recorded separately in the index and development ledger.
No live LinkedIn qualification, human acceptance, observed file-browser window,
commit, push or new release is claimed.

